INSERT INTO `wp_posts` VALUES (1,1,'2026-09-21 11:57:16','2026-09-21 11:57:16','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Welcome to WordPress. This is your first post. Edit or delete it, then start writing!</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->','Hello world!','','publish','open','open','','hello-world','','','2026-09-21 11:57:16','2026-09-21 11:57:16','',0,'/?p=1',0,'post','',1),(2,1,'2026-09-21 11:57:16','2026-09-21 11:57:16','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This is an example page. It\'s different from a blog post because it will stay in one place and will show up in your site navigation (in most themes). Most people start with an About page that introduces them to potential site visitors. It might say something like this:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\">\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Hi there! I\'m a bike messenger by day, aspiring actor by night, and this is my website. I live in Los Angeles, have a great dog named Jack, and I like pi&#241;a coladas. (And gettin\' caught in the rain.)</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n</blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>...or something like this:</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\">\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The XYZ Doohickey Company was founded in 1971, and has been providing quality doohickeys to the public ever since. Located in Gotham City, XYZ employs over 2,000 people and does all kinds of awesome things for the Gotham community.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n</blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>As a new WordPress user, you should go to <a href=\"/wp-admin/\">your dashboard</a> to delete this page and create new pages for your content. Have fun!</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->','Sample Page','','publish','closed','open','','sample-page','','','2026-09-21 11:57:16','2026-09-21 11:57:16','',0,'/?page_id=2',0,'page','',0),(3,1,'2026-09-21 11:57:16','2026-09-21 11:57:16','<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Who we are</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong class=\"privacy-policy-tutorial\">Suggested text: </strong>Our website address is: .</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Comments</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong class=\"privacy-policy-tutorial\">Suggested text: </strong>When visitors leave comments on the site we collect the data shown in the comments form, and also the visitor&#8217;s IP address and browser user agent string to help spam detection.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>An anonymized string created from your email address (also called a hash) may be provided to the Gravatar service to see if you are using it. The Gravatar service privacy policy is available here: https://automattic.com/privacy/. After approval of your comment, your profile picture is visible to the public in the context of your comment.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Media</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong class=\"privacy-policy-tutorial\">Suggested text: </strong>If you upload images to the website, you should avoid uploading images with embedded location data (EXIF GPS) included. Visitors to the website can download and extract any location data from images on the website.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Cookies</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong class=\"privacy-policy-tutorial\">Suggested text: </strong>If you leave a comment on our site you may opt-in to saving your name, email address and website in cookies. These are for your convenience so that you do not have to fill in your details again when you leave another comment. These cookies will last for one year.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If you visit our login page, we will set a temporary cookie to determine if your browser accepts cookies. This cookie contains no personal data and is discarded when you close your browser.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>When you log in, we will also set up several cookies to save your login information and your screen display choices. Login cookies last for two days, and screen options cookies last for a year. If you select &quot;Remember Me&quot;, your login will persist for two weeks. If you log out of your account, the login cookies will be removed.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If you edit or publish an article, an additional cookie will be saved in your browser. This cookie includes no personal data and simply indicates the post ID of the article you just edited. It expires after 1 day.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Embedded content from other websites</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong class=\"privacy-policy-tutorial\">Suggested text: </strong>Articles on this site may include embedded content (e.g. videos, images, articles, etc.). Embedded content from other websites behaves in the exact same way as if the visitor has visited the other website.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>These websites may collect data about you, use cookies, embed additional third-party tracking, and monitor your interaction with that embedded content, including tracking your interaction with the embedded content if you have an account and are logged in to that website.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Who we share your data with</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong class=\"privacy-policy-tutorial\">Suggested text: </strong>If you request a password reset, your IP address will be included in the reset email.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">How long we retain your data</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong class=\"privacy-policy-tutorial\">Suggested text: </strong>If you leave a comment, the comment and its metadata are retained indefinitely. This is so we can recognize and approve any follow-up comments automatically instead of holding them in a moderation queue.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For users that register on our website (if any), we also store the personal information they provide in their user profile. All users can see, edit, or delete their personal information at any time (except they cannot change their username). Website administrators can also see and edit that information.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What rights you have over your data</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong class=\"privacy-policy-tutorial\">Suggested text: </strong>If you have an account on this site, or have left comments, you can request to receive an exported file of the personal data we hold about you, including any data you have provided to us. You can also request that we erase any personal data we hold about you. This does not include any data we are obliged to keep for administrative, legal, or security purposes.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Where your data is sent</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p><strong class=\"privacy-policy-tutorial\">Suggested text: </strong>Visitor comments may be checked through an automated spam detection service.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Privacy Policy','','draft','closed','open','','privacy-policy','','','2026-09-21 11:57:16','2026-09-21 11:57:16','',0,'/?page_id=3',0,'page','',0),(4,1,'2026-09-21 12:33:40','2026-09-21 12:33:40','<p class=\"dropcap\">Federal Reserve officials signaled today that a September rate reduction is firmly on the table following a notable cooling in the U.S. labour market and steady progress toward their 2% inflation target.</p><p>Investors reacted swiftly to the release of the policy meeting summary, pushing two-year Treasury yields down 8 basis points while broad equity indices rallied across North America and Europe.</p><blockquote class=\"pullquote\">“The signal here is clearer than the market had assumed entering this meeting.”</blockquote><p>Market participants are now pricing in a 25 basis point rate cut, with swap markets indicating a total of 75 basis points of monetary easing before the end of the year.</p>','Fed Signals Rate Cut in September as US Labour Market Cools','Federal Reserve policymakers indicated that an interest rate reduction is likely at their next meeting as inflation moderates.','publish','open','open','','fed-signals-rate-cut-in-september-as-us-labour-market-cools','','','2026-09-21 12:33:40','2026-09-21 12:33:40','',0,'http://localhost/financial/?p=4',0,'post','',0),(5,1,'2026-09-21 12:33:40','2026-09-21 12:33:40','<p class=\"dropcap\">The S&amp;P 500 reached a fresh record high on Thursday as major chipmakers and cloud infrastructure providers reported robust quarterly earnings fueled by enterprise artificial intelligence investments.</p><p>Nvidia, Microsoft, and Alphabet led gains, contributing to a 1.2% rise in the tech-heavy Nasdaq Composite.</p><p>Analysts noted that enterprise AI spending shows no signs of decelerating, with guidance updates exceeding Wall Street estimates across the semiconductor supply chain.</p>','S&amp;P 500 Hits Record High Driven by Strong Tech Earnings and AI Demand','Wall Street benchmark surges as mega-cap technology firms report stronger-than-expected revenue and cloud growth.','publish','open','open','','sp-500-hits-record-high-driven-by-strong-tech-earnings-and-ai-demand','','','2026-09-21 12:33:40','2026-09-21 12:33:40','',0,'http://localhost/financial/?p=5',0,'post','',0),(6,1,'2026-09-21 12:33:40','2026-09-21 12:33:40','<p class=\"dropcap\">Oil prices retreated on global energy markets after delegates from OPEC+ indicated the cartel is reviewing proposals to phase out voluntary production cuts starting in the fourth quarter.</p><p>Brent crude fell 1.3% to settle at $74.62 per barrel, while West Texas Intermediate touched a three-week low.</p>','Oil Prices Slip as OPEC+ Considers Unwinding Output Restrictions','Brent crude futures dropped below $78 a barrel on reports that OPEC+ member nations may gradually restore supply.','publish','open','open','','oil-prices-slip-as-opec-considers-unwinding-output-restrictions','','','2026-09-21 12:33:40','2026-09-21 12:33:40','',0,'http://localhost/financial/?p=6',0,'post','',0),(7,1,'2026-09-21 12:33:40','2026-09-21 12:33:40','<p class=\"dropcap\">The US Dollar index edged lower against major peers as market participants adjusted positions ahead of the Bank of Japan’s upcoming policy announcement.</p><p>The Yen strengthened toward 146.20 against the dollar amid growing expectations that Japanese monetary authorities could adjust policy settings.</p>','Dollar Softens Ahead of Bank of Japan Interest Rate Decision','Currency markets trade cautiously as traders weigh potential monetary tightening from Japan’s central bank.','publish','open','open','','dollar-softens-ahead-of-bank-of-japan-interest-rate-decision','','','2026-09-21 12:33:40','2026-09-21 12:33:40','',0,'http://localhost/financial/?p=7',0,'post','',0),(8,1,'2026-09-21 12:33:40','2026-09-21 12:33:40','<p class=\"dropcap\">The private credit market has exploded over the past decade into a $2 trillion asset class, attracting pension funds, sovereign wealth funds, and private wealth clients seeking yields.</p><p>While non-bank lending offers flexible financing structures, market observers are closely scrutinizing covenants and liquidity terms in an environment of higher borrowing costs.</p>','Inside the $2tn Private Credit Surge: Risks and Opportunities for Investors','Rapid growth in direct lending has reshaped corporate finance, creating new dynamics for institutional portfolios.','publish','open','open','','inside-the-2tn-private-credit-surge-risks-and-opportunities-for-investors','','','2026-09-21 12:33:40','2026-09-21 12:33:40','',0,'http://localhost/financial/?p=8',0,'post','',0),(9,1,'2026-09-21 12:39:23','0000-00-00 00:00:00','','Auto Draft','','auto-draft','open','open','','','','','2026-09-21 12:39:23','0000-00-00 00:00:00','',0,'http://localhost/financial/?p=9',0,'post','',0),(10,1,'2026-09-24 06:15:50','0000-00-00 00:00:00','<h1>Example Domain</h1>\n<p>This domain is for use in documentation examples without needing permission. Avoid use in operations.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://iana.org/domains/example\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Learn more</a></p>\n\n\n<hr><p class=\"text-muted small\"><em>Source: <a href=\"https://example.com\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">https://example.com</a> (Crawled via Crawl4AI)</em></p>','Example Domain','','draft','open','open','','','','','2026-09-24 06:15:50','0000-00-00 00:00:00','',0,'http://localhost/financial/?p=10',0,'post','',0),(11,1,'2026-09-24 09:06:51','0000-00-00 00:00:00','<p>[Skip to content](https://techcrunch.com/events/techcrunch-disrupt/?utm_source=google&amp;utm_medium=ad&amp;utm_campaign=disrupt2026&amp;utm_content=addiscount&amp;promo=Disrupt25&amp;display=true#wp--skip-link--target)</p>\n<p>01:21:53:13</p>\n<p>Last day to save up to $200 + 50% off a second pass ends Sept 25, 11:59 p.m. PT.</p>\n<p>Bring your community with up to 30% discount.</p>\n<p>Close</p>\n<p>–:–:–:–</p>\n<p>Last day to save up to $200 + 50% off a second pass ends Sept 25, 11:59 p.m. PT.</p>\n<p>Bring your community with up to 30% discount.</p>\n<p>Close</p>\n<p>Innovation for Every Stage</p>\n<p>**Image Credits:** TC</p>\n<p>Disrupt is where you’ll find innovation for every stage of your startup journey. Whether you’re a budding founder with a revolutionary idea, a seasoned startup looking to scale, or an investor seeking the next big thing, Disrupt offers unparalleled resources, connections, and expert insights to propel your venture forward.</p>\n<p>Last day to save up to $200 on September 25, 11:59 p.m. PT</p>\n<p>* 10,000+ Attendees</p>\n<p>* $32B Raised by Startup Battlefield alumni</p>\n<p>* 20,000+ Connections Made</p>\n<h3>Who should attend?</h3>\n<h3>Founders</h3>\n<p>Scale faster through funding, exposure, networking, and startup growth insights.</p>\n<h3>Investors</h3>\n<p>Grow your portfolio by discovering breakout startups, emerging trends, and founders.</p>\n<h3>Operators</h3>\n<p>Gain practical strategies, peer insights, and tools to scale operations effectively.</p>\n<h3>Innovators</h3>\n<p>Explore breakthrough ideas, emerging tech, and connections shaping the future.</p>\n<h3>Featured Speakers</h3>\n<h3>Mark Wahlberg</h3>\n<p>President Music for the People</p>\n<p>View Profile</p>\n<p>[Beyond the Check: The Power of Access](https://techcrunch.com/events/techcrunch-disrupt/agenda/#session-beyond-the-check-the-power-of-access?promo=Disrupt25&amp;utm_campaign=disrupt2026&amp;utm_content=addiscount&amp;utm_medium=ad&amp;utm_source=google)</p>\n<h3>RJ Scaringe</h3>\n<p>Founder and CEO Rivian</p>\n<p>View Profile</p>\n<p>[Building the Impossible](https://techcrunch.com/events/techcrunch-disrupt/agenda/#session-building-the-impossible?promo=Disrupt25&amp;utm_campaign=disrupt2026&amp;utm_content=addiscount&amp;utm_medium=ad&amp;utm_source=google)</p>\n<h3>Alexa von Tobel</h3>\n<p>Founder and Managing Partner Inspired Capital</p>\n<p>View Profile</p>\n<p>[Startup Battlefield Final](https://techcrunch.com/events/techcrunch-disrupt/agenda/#session-startup-battlefield-final?promo=Disrupt25&amp;utm_campaign=disrupt2026&amp;utm_content=addiscount&amp;utm_medium=ad&amp;utm_source=google)</p>\n<h3>Garrett Langley</h3>\n<p>CEO and Co-founder Flock</p>\n<p>View Profile</p>\n<p>[AI, Surveillance, and the Future of Public Safety](https://techcrunch.com/events/techcrunch-disrupt/agenda/#session-ai-surveillance-and-the-future-of-public-safety?promo=Disrupt25&amp;utm_campaign=disrupt2026&amp;utm_content=addiscount&amp;utm_medium=ad&amp;utm_source=google)</p>\n<h3>Panos Panay</h3>\n<p>SVP, Devices, Alexa &amp; Leo Amazon</p>\n<p>View Profile</p>\n<p>[What Comes After the Smartphone? ](https://techcrunch.com/events/techcrunch-disrupt/agenda/#session-what-comes-after-the-smartphone?promo=Disrupt25&amp;utm_campaign=disrupt2026&amp;utm_content=addiscount&amp;utm_medium=ad&amp;utm_source=google)</p>\n<h3>Anna Goldie</h3>\n<p>Founder &amp; CEO Ricursive Intelligence</p>\n<p>View Profile</p>\n<p>[When AI Starts Designing Its Own Hardware](https://techcrunch.com/events/techcrunch-disrupt/agenda/#session-when-ai-starts-designing-its-own-hardware?promo=Disrupt25&amp;utm_campaign=disrupt2026&amp;utm_content=addiscount&amp;utm_medium=ad&amp;utm_source=google)</p>\n<h3>Andrew Feldman</h3>\n<p>CEO and Co-founder Cerebras Systems</p>\n<p>View Profile</p>\n<p>[Can AI Keep Scaling? ](https://techcrunch.com/events/techcrunch-disrupt/agenda/#session-can-ai-keep-scaling?promo=Disrupt25&amp;utm_campaign=disrupt2026&amp;utm_content=addiscount&amp;utm_medium=ad&amp;utm_source=google)</p>\n<h3>Amjad Masad</h3>\n<p>CEO and Co-founder Replit</p>\n<p>View Profile</p>\n<p>[Everyone Can Build Software Now. What Happens Next? ](https://techcrunch.com/events/techcrunch-disrupt/agenda/#session-everyone-can-build-software-now-what-happens-next?promo=Disrupt25&amp;utm_campaign=disrupt2026&amp;utm_content=addiscount&amp;utm_medium=ad&amp;utm_source=google)</p>\n<h3>Toni Schneider</h3>\n<p>CEO Bluesky Social</p>\n<p>View Profile</p>\n<p>[Can Social Start Over? ](https://techcrunch.com/events/techcrunch-disrupt/agenda/#session-can-social-start-over?promo=Disrupt25&amp;utm_campaign=disrupt2026&amp;utm_content=addiscount&amp;utm_medium=ad&amp;utm_source=google)</p>\n<h3>Disrupt news and updates</h3>\n<p>* ###  [StrictlyVC at TechCrunch Disrupt 2026: Inside the changing rules of venture capital](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/23/strictlyvc-at-techcrunch-disrupt-2026-inside-the-changing-rules-of-venture-capital/)</p>\n<p>19 hours ago</p>\n<p>* ###  [3 days left to save up to $200 and make impactful connections at TechCrunch Disrupt 2026](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/23/3-days-left-to-save-up-to-200-and-make-impactful-connections-at-techcrunch-disrupt-2026/)</p>\n<p>19 hours ago</p>\n<p>* ###  [Five AI safety sessions every founder should have on their TechCrunch Disrupt 2026 agenda](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/22/five-ai-safety-sessions-every-founder-should-have-on-their-techcrunch-disrupt-2026-agenda/)</p>\n<p>2 days ago</p>\n<p>* ###  [StrictlyVC at TechCrunch Disrupt 2026: Inside the changing rules of venture capital](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/23/strictlyvc-at-techcrunch-disrupt-2026-inside-the-changing-rules-of-venture-capital/)</p>\n<p>19 hours ago</p>\n<p>* ###  [3 days left to save up to $200 and make impactful connections at TechCrunch Disrupt 2026](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/23/3-days-left-to-save-up-to-200-and-make-impactful-connections-at-techcrunch-disrupt-2026/)</p>\n<p>19 hours ago</p>\n<p>* ###  [Five AI safety sessions every founder should have on their TechCrunch Disrupt 2026 agenda](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/22/five-ai-safety-sessions-every-founder-should-have-on-their-techcrunch-disrupt-2026-agenda/)</p>\n<p>2 days ago</p>\n<h3>Key Topics</h3>\n<h3>Building &amp; Scaling</h3>\n<p>Practical insights for founders on fundraising, company building, scaling operations, and maintaining a founder-first approach while growing a business.</p>\n<h3>AI &amp; the Physical World</h3>\n<p>Focus on how AI is expanding in software and beyond such as robotics, autonomous systems, industrial operations, energy, climate tech, and scientific discovery.</p>\n<h3>Digital Finance</h3>\n<p>Discover how stablecoins, embedded finance, and digital payment infrastructure are transforming the future of money movement.</p>\n<h3>Machines, Infrastructure &amp; Energy</h3>\n<p>Learn how the next generation of founders is rebuilding physical-world infrastructure across energy, climate, manufacturing, and industrial systems.</p>\n<h3>Tickets</h3>\n<p>Whether you’re attending solo or bringing your team, there’s a pass designed for you.</p>\n<p>Save up to $200 when you register before September 25 at 11:59 p.m. PT and get 50% off a second ticket for your colleague on select ticket types. Or if you’re bringing a group, enjoy up to 30% off.</p>\n<p>Save $200</p>\n<p>Attendee Pass</p>\n<p>$849</p>\n<p>Regular price $1,049</p>\n<p>Buy Now</p>\n<p>Show pass perks</p>\n<p>Save $200</p>\n<p>Founder Pass</p>\n<p>$899</p>\n<p>Regular price $1,099</p>\n<p>Buy Now</p>\n<p>Save $200</p>\n<p>Investor Pass</p>\n<p>$999</p>\n<p>Regular price $1,199</p>\n<p>Buy Now</p>\n<p>Show pass perks</p>\n<h3>Featured Sessions</h3>\n<p>October 13  10:30AM – 11:10AM</p>\n<p>Builders Stage  Hiring + Talent</p>\n<h3>Competing for AI Talent: Pay, Equity, and Retention</h3>\n<p>Founder Wylder.co</p>\n<p>Co-founder Runware</p>\n<p>VP, Talent Network Redpoint Ventures</p>\n<p>The growth of AI startups has made hiring and retention more difficult for every tech company. From competing for AI talent to navigating secondary sa…</p>\n<p>October 14  10:00AM – 10:40AM</p>\n<p>Industry Stage – Real World AI  Artificial Intelligence, Robotics + Physical AI</p>\n<h3>Operating at the Edge: How AI Works When the Cloud Doesn’t</h3>\n<p>CEO and Founder FieldAI</p>\n<p>Founder and CEO Medra</p>\n<p>Partner Eclipse</p>\n<p>The most valuable AI deployments in the world operate where the cloud can’t reach. And building for them requires a different technological playbook. …</p>\n<p>October 15  1:00PM – 1:40PM</p>\n<p>Builders Stage  Go-to-Market + Sales</p>\n<h3>The First 90 Days: How Startups Build Toward $10M ARR</h3>\n<p>GTM Leader Lovable</p>\n<p>Founder Polsia</p>\n<p>General Partner and Founder Theory Ventures</p>\n<p>The definition of traction has changed. What once took years is now expected in months, and $0–$10M ARR is increasingly becoming the new early-stage b…</p>\n<p>* &gt; I would say that Disrupt is a pivotal experience when we’ve been fundraising.</p>\n<p>Startup that gained massive investor inbound from connections made at Disrupt</p>\n<p>* &gt; First time I pitched to investors — they corrected my pitch and reviewed my presentation. It was super insightful for me.</p>\n<p>Founder who left with new investors and actionable feedback</p>\n<p>* &gt; As an investor, I would say my experience at Disrupt has been outstanding. The hospitality in the founder and investor room was also great. I bumped into a lot of people there without planning anything.</p>\n<p>An investor who connected with founders in the Deal Flow Cafe</p>\n<h3>Platinum Partners</h3>\n<p>TechCrunch offers many ways for partners to engage directly with our attendees before, during, and after the event. If you’re interested in learning more about how your company can be part of the event, get in touch with our sales team below.</p>\n<p>* [ Google for Startups ](https://startup.google.com/)</p>\n<p>* [ Schneider Electric ](https://www.se.com/ww/en/)</p>\n<p>* [ The Aerospace Corporation ](https://aerospace.org/)</p>\n<p>* [ BLACKBOX AI ](https://www.blackbox.ai/)</p>\n<p>* [ Fidelity Private Shares ](https://www.fidelityprivateshares.com/schedule-demo?utm_campaign=44722268-2026-TechCrunchDisrupt&amp;utm_source=industry-event&amp;utm_medium=techcrunch-disrupt&amp;utm_term=schedule-demo&amp;utm_content=schedule-demo)</p>\n<p>* [ Greenfield Partners ](https://www.greenfield-growth.com/)</p>\n<p>* [ Invest in Türkiye ](https://www.invest.gov.tr)</p>\n<p>* [ KOTRA ](https://www.kotra.or.kr/english/index.do)</p>\n<p>* [ The Polish Investment and Trade Agency ](https://www.paih.gov.pl/en/)</p>\n<p>* [ RoMADE – Romanian Mobile Apps Developers and Experts ](https://romade.org/)</p>\n<p>* [ SEW.AI ](https://www.SEW.AI)</p>\n<p>* [ Signify Business Cash World Elite Mastercard® by Wells Fargo ](https://www.wellsfargo.com/signify)</p>\n<p>* [ Silkroad Innovation Hub ](https://silkroadinnovationhub.com)</p>\n<p>* [ TriNet ](https://www.trinet.com/eventscontactus?utm_source=event&amp;utm_medium=learnmore&amp;utm_campaign=TechCrunchDisrupt&amp;campaign_id=701Nr000012A73WIAS&amp;lead_source=marketing-event-sponsorship)</p>\n<p>* [ Upper Silesian Fund ](https://fgsa.pl/en/)</p>\n<h3>[STARTUP BATTLEFIELD](https://techcrunch.com/startup-battlefield/)</h3>\n<p>Top-tier expert judges. World-renowned past winners. Free and open participation. The world’s top early stage startups together on one stage to compete for equity free prize money, and the attention of media and investors world wide.</p>\n<p>Featured</p>\n<h3>[The 2026 Startup Battlefield 200 is here — see who made the cut](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/20/the-2026-startup-battlefield-200-is-here-see-who-made-the-cut/)</h3>\n<p>Aug 20, 2026</p>\n<h3>**Upcoming Event** s</h3>\n<p>* ###  [TechCrunch Founder Summit 2026](https://techcrunch.com/events/techcrunch-founder-summit-2026/?promo=Disrupt25&amp;utm_campaign=disrupt2026&amp;utm_content=addiscount&amp;utm_medium=ad&amp;utm_source=google)</p>\n<p>Boston, Massachusetts  November 4, 2026</p>\n<p>Presented by</p>\n<p>Grab your pass at a low rate</p>\n<h3>Subscribe</h3>\n<p>#### Stay connected</p>\n<p>Get the latest event announcements, special discounts and other event offers.</p>\n<p>[Explore all newsletters ](https://techcrunch.com/newsletters/)</p>\n<p>Previous</p>\n<p>Close</p>\n<p>Mark Wahlberg</p>\n<p>President, Music for the People</p>\n<p>* [ Twitter ](https://x.com/markwahlberg)</p>\n<p>Mark Wahlberg is a globally recognized actor, producer, entrepreneur, and philanthropist whose career spans over three decades. Renowned for his versatility and excellence in entertainment, he has earned two Academy Award nominations and built an enduring legacy both on-screen and behind the scenes. Committed to giving back, Wahlberg founded the Mark Wahlberg Youth Foundation in 2001 to support inner-city children and teens. His dedication to philanthropy reflects a lifelong mission to create meaningful and lasting change in underserved communities. From his award-winning entertainment career to his thriving business ventures and philanthropic efforts, Mark Wahlberg continues to exemplify innovation, leadership, and purpose in every endeavor.</p>\n<p>#### Mark&#039;s Sessions</p>\n<p>##### Beyond the Check: The Power of Access</p>\n<p>Mark Wahlberg built his career by spotting opportunities before others did. Now, alongside Bruce Lee, Senior advisor to the Wahlberg family office and business affairs, he’s applying those same instincts to venture investing while learning the discipline required to compete at an institutional level. Join them for a conversation about what influence can and can’t buy, how intuition complements data in investment decisions, and why they’re betting on health technology as the next chapter of their investing journey.</p>\n<p>October 15  15:45PM – 16:15PM America/Los_Angeles  Disrupt Stage  Markets + Investing</p>\n<p>[ View in Agenda ](https://techcrunch.com/events/techcrunch-disrupt/agenda/#session-beyond-the-check-the-power-of-access?promo=Disrupt25&amp;utm_campaign=disrupt2026&amp;utm_content=addiscount&amp;utm_medium=ad&amp;utm_source=google)</p>\n<p>RJ Scaringe</p>\n<p>Founder and CEO, Rivian</p>\n<p>* [ Twitter ](https://twitter.com/rjscaringe?lang=en)</p>\n<p>* [ Linkedin ](https://www.instagram.com/rjscaringe/)</p>\n<p>RJ Scaringe founded Rivian in 2009 to redefine the future of transportation through high-performance electric vehicles designed to enhance grid stability and long-term energy efficiency. He has led the company to establish vertically integrated technology platforms, build multi-program manufacturing capabilities, and set forth a strategic vision that will drive profound change in the transportation industry. Today, Rivian’s consumer products include the software-defined R1S, R1T, R2, and Rivian Commercial Van. In the commercial space, Rivian is fulfilling Amazon’s historic order for 100,000 electric delivery vans and recently partnered with Uber to deploy up to 50,000 autonomous robotaxis. In 2025, RJ expanded this vision beyond traditional automotive by founding Mind Robotics, an AI-enabled industrial robotics company building a world model and robot platform capable of performing complex industrial and manufacturing tasks at scale. Earlier in 2025, he co-founded ALSO, an electric mobility company spun out of Rivian to build right-sized vehicles for the autonomous delivery era. Across these organizations, RJ operates from the belief that the only way to build the kind of world our kids’ kids deserve is by building teams obsessed with tackling hard problems and fostering collaboration across industries and society.</p>\n<p>#### RJ&#039;s Sessions</p>\n<p>##### Building the Impossible</p>\n<p>Building a breakthrough software company is hard. Building one that also designs, manufactures, and delivers world-class hardware is even harder. Rivian founder and CEO RJ Scaringe joins us to discuss what it takes to build an enduring company at the intersection of AI, software, robotics, manufacturing, and transportation.</p>\n<p>October 13  11:15AM – 11:45AM America/Los_Angeles  Disrupt Stage  Leadership + Scaling</p>\n<p>[ View in Agenda ](https://techcrunch.com/events/techcrunch-disrupt/agenda/#session-building-the-impossible?promo=Disrupt25&amp;utm_campaign=disrupt2026&amp;utm_content=addiscount&amp;utm_medium=ad&amp;utm_source=google)</p>\n<p>Alexa von Tobel</p>\n<p>Founder and Managing Partner, Inspired Capital</p>\n<p>* [ Twitter ](https://x.com/alexavontobel)</p>\n<p>* [ Facebook ](http://www.facebook.com/alexavontobel)</p>\n<p>* [ Linkedin ](https://www.linkedin.com/in/alexavontobel)</p>\n<p>Alexa founded Inspired Capital because she deeply understood the unique challenges of the founder journey. Before Inspired, she dropped out of Harvard Business School to launch LearnVest, one of the first fintech companies aimed at making a financial planning software accessible to millions. Discouraged by the capital partners available in NYC at the time, Alexa promised herself she would one day become the kind of investor she wished had existed. In 2015, LearnVest was acquired for nearly $400 million, marking one of the decade’s largest fintech acquisitions. Alexa went on to establish herself as an industry authority and New York Times bestselling author, with three books on financial planning. Born in Kentucky and raised in Florida, Alexa attended Harvard College, where she earned an AB in Psychology with Honors. Her career began on Morgan Stanley’s Proprietary Trading Desk, before helping to build Drop.io, an early-stage startup that was later acquired by Facebook. After LearnVest’s acquisition, Alexa became Northwestern Mutual’s first-ever Chief Digital Officer, leading product and technology strategy as the company achieved $30 billion in annual revenue. She later became Chief Innovation Officer, overseeing Northwestern Mutual’s venture arm. Alexa is a 2016 Henry Crown Fellow and an inaugural member of President Obama’s Ambassadors for Global Entrepreneurship, where she first met her co-founder, Penny Pritzker. Her achievements have earned her numerous accolades, including a Forbes cover, Fortune’s 40 Under 40, Most Powerful Women, Inc. Magazine’s 30 Under 30, and the title of World Economic Forum Young Global Leader. She is also the New York Times bestselling author of Financially Fearless, Financially Forward, and Money Matters. Alexa lives in New York City with her husband, Cliff Ryan, who leads Galvanize Climate Solutions’ venture and growth strategy, one of the world’s leading climate tech funds. Together, they are raising three children.</p>\n<p>#### Alexa&#039;s Sessions</p>\n<p>##### Startup Battlefield Final</p>\n<p>The grand finale of TechCrunch’s iconic startup competition. Watch the final 5 teams from around the world pitch expert judges and vie for the Battlefield Cup and $100,000.</p>\n<p>October 15  11:30AM – 12:45PM America/Los_Angeles  Disrupt Stage  Fundraising, Venture Capital</p>\n<p>[ View in Agenda ](https://techcrunch.com/events/techcrunch-disrupt/agenda/#session-startup-battlefield-final?promo=Disrupt25&amp;utm_campaign=disrupt2026&amp;utm_content=addiscount&amp;utm_medium=ad&amp;utm_source=google)</p>\n<p>##### Ask the Investors</p>\n<p>What makes an investor say yes? In this audience-led Q&amp;A, the Startup Battlefield finals judges take your toughest questions on what separates a fundable startup from the rest: team, traction, market opportunity, pitch delivery, red flags, and more. Come ready to ask and get candid answers straight from the investors making the decisions.</p>\n<p>October 15  14:50PM – 15:30PM America/Los_Angeles  Builders Stage</p>\n<p>[ View in Agenda ](https://techcrunch.com/events/techcrunch-disrupt/agenda/#session-ask-the-investors?promo=Disrupt25&amp;utm_campaign=disrupt2026&amp;utm_content=addiscount&amp;utm_medium=ad&amp;utm_source=google)</p>\n<p>Garrett Langley</p>\n<p>CEO and Co-founder, Flock</p>\n<p>* [ Twitter ](https://x.com/Flock_Safety)</p>\n<p>* [ Linkedin ](https://www.linkedin.com/in/glangley/)</p>\n<p>Garrett Langley is a technology entrepreneur who has dedicated the last decade of his career to the bold mission of eliminating crime and building thriving communities. Garrett founded and leads Flock Safety, the first all-in-one technology platform pioneering a new way for communities to think about public safety — with efficacy, affordability, transparency, and accountability in mind. Flock Safety was founded following Garrett’s own experience with crime in his neighborhood. Upon approaching law enforcement to provide evidence and secure justice, he learned how existing neighborhood security solutions were simply not effective in providing actionable evidence that solves crime. An electrical engineer and serial technology entrepreneur, Garrett, along with co-founders Matt Feury and Paige Todd, founded Flock Safety in 2017 to transform the nascent public safety technology space with the most advanced technology, unwavering ethical commitments, and an inclusive community-forward approach. Flock Safety’s transformative mission follows from Garrett’s experience growing successful companies that reimagined traditional sectors: Clutch, a monthly car subscription service that allowed consumers to swap their vehicle for any occasion, and Experience, a mobile-first fan experience platform that allowed users to upgrade live events. Overseeing the engineering, design, product, data science, and customer support groups at Experience, Garrett led the sale of the company to Cox Enterprises for $200 million in 2014. Outside of work, Garrett dedicates his time to inspire future engineers to build technology that serves as a force for good. Garrett sits on several boards through his alma mater, the Georgia Institute of Technology, including the Advisory Board for the Georgia Tech School of Electrical and Computer Engineering, the Georgia Tech Athletic Association Alexander Tharpe Board, and the Board of Directors of Georgia Advanced Technology Ventures (GATV), which supports Georgia Tech’s economic development and innovation initiatives. He also serves on the Metro Atlanta Chamber 2023 Board of Directors, the Board of the High Museum, and the Board of the Atlanta History Center. Garrett lives in Atlanta with his wife, Megan, his children, and his dog.</p>\n<p>#### Garrett&#039;s Sessions</p>\n<p>##### AI, Surveillance, and the Future of Public Safety</p>\n<p>As AI moves from the cloud into the real world, few companies sit at the center of the debate quite like Flock Safety. Join founder and CEO Garrett Langley for a conversation about privacy, security, public safety, and the difficult trade-offs that emerge when AI becomes part of the infrastructure of everyday life.</p>\n<p>October 14  11:15AM – 11:45AM America/Los_Angeles  Disrupt Stage  Artificial Intelligence</p>\n<p>[ View in Agenda ](https://techcrunch.com/events/techcrunch-disrupt/agenda/#session-ai-surveillance-and-the-future-of-public-safety?promo=Disrupt25&amp;utm_campaign=disrupt2026&amp;utm_content=addiscount&amp;utm_medium=ad&amp;utm_source=google)</p>\n<p>Panos Panay</p>\n<p>SVP, Devices, Alexa &amp; Leo, Amazon</p>\n<p>* [ Twitter ](https://x.com/panos_panay)</p>\n<p>* [ Linkedin ](https://www.linkedin.com/in/panos-panay-22b60ba9/)</p>\n<p>Panos Panay joined Amazon in October 2023 as Senior Vice President, Devices, Alexa &amp; Leo. He leads Amazon’s consumer electronics devices and the services behind them—including Alexa+, Echo, Kindle, Fire TV, tablets, Ring, eero, Blink, Bee, Amazon Photos, and more—as well as other initiatives such as Amazon Leo and Zoox. Previously, Panos served as Executive Vice President &amp; Chief Product Officer at Microsoft, where he led the Windows + Devices businesses. Panos holds an MBA from Pepperdine University and a Bachelor of Science from California State University, Northridge.</p>\n<p>#### Panos&#039;s Sessions</p>\n<p>##### What Comes After the Smartphone?</p>\n<p>For nearly two decades, the smartphone has been the center of our digital lives. But as AI transforms how we interact with technology, the next generation of products may look — and behave — very differently. Join Amazon’s Panos Panay for a conversation about AI, ambient computing, and the future of the devices we’ll use every day.</p>\n<p>October 15  10:00AM – 10:30AM America/Los_Angeles  Disrupt Stage  Consumer Technology</p>\n<p>[ View in Agenda ](https://techcrunch.com/events/techcrunch-disrupt/agenda/#session-what-comes-after-the-smartphone?promo=Disrupt25&amp;utm_campaign=disrupt2026&amp;utm_content=addiscount&amp;utm_medium=ad&amp;utm_source=google)</p>\n<p>Anna Goldie</p>\n<p>Founder &amp; CEO, Ricursive Intelligence</p>\n<p>* [ Twitter ](https://x.com/annadgoldie?s=20)</p>\n<p>* [ Linkedin ](https://www.linkedin.com/in/adgoldie/)</p>\n<p>Anna Goldie is Founder and CEO of Ricursive Intelligence. She previously co-led the AlphaChip project, and co-founded the ML for Systems team at Google. She was an early employee at Anthropic and a Senior Staff Research Scientist at Google DeepMind, where she developed frontier models such as Claude and Gemini. At MIT, she double majored in EECS and Linguistics and earned a Masters in EECS. She also holds a CS PhD from Stanford. She was named one of MIT Technology Review’s 35 Innovators Under 35, and her research has been widely covered in major media outlets and published in Nature, NeurIPS, ICLR, EMNLP, COLM, ISPD, ICLAD, MLCAD, MLSys, and ASPLOS.</p>\n<p>#### Anna&#039;s Sessions</p>\n<p>##### When AI Starts Designing Its Own Hardware</p>\n<p>AI’s progress depends on better chips, but what happens when AI begins designing the hardware that powers it? Ricursive Intelligence co-founders Dr. Anna Goldie and Dr. Azalia Mirhoseini discuss closing the loop between AI and chip development, building self-improving systems, and tackling one of the most consequential bottlenecks in the race toward more powerful intelligence.</p>\n<p>October 14  15:15PM – 15:45PM America/Los_Angeles  Disrupt Stage  Engineering</p>\n<p>[ View in Agenda ](https://techcrunch.com/events/techcrunch-disrupt/agenda/#session-when-ai-starts-designing-its-own-hardware?promo=Disrupt25&amp;utm_campaign=disrupt2026&amp;utm_content=addiscount&amp;utm_medium=ad&amp;utm_source=google)</p>\n<p>Andrew Feldman</p>\n<p>CEO and Co-founder, Cerebras Systems</p>\n<p>* [ Twitter ](https://x.com/andrewdfeldman)</p>\n<p>* [ Linkedin ](https://www.linkedin.com/in/andrewdfeldman/)</p>\n<p>Andrew is co-founder and CEO of Cerebras Systems. He is an entrepreneur dedicated to pushing boundaries in the compute space. Prior to Cerebras, he co-founded and was CEO of SeaMicro, a pioneer of energy-efficient, high-bandwidth microservers. SeaMicro was acquired by AMD in 2012 for $357M. Before SeaMicro, Andrew was the Vice President of Product Management, Marketing and BD at Force10 Networks which was later sold to Dell Computing for $800M. Prior to Force10 Networks, Andrew was the Vice President of Marketing and Corporate Development at RiverStone Networks from the company’s inception through IPO in 2001. Andrew holds a BA and an MBA from Stanford University.</p>\n<p>#### Andrew&#039;s Sessions</p>\n<p>##### Can AI Keep Scaling?</p>\n<p>The AI boom has created an unprecedented demand for compute, energy, and infrastructure. Cerebras is challenging conventional assumptions about how AI systems should be built and what comes next if today’s hardware reaches its limits. Join founder and CEO Andrew Feldman for a conversation about the future of AI at scale.</p>\n<p>October 13  14:15PM – 14:45PM America/Los_Angeles  Disrupt Stage  Artificial Intelligence</p>\n<p>[ View in Agenda ](https://techcrunch.com/events/techcrunch-disrupt/agenda/#session-can-ai-keep-scaling?promo=Disrupt25&amp;utm_campaign=disrupt2026&amp;utm_content=addiscount&amp;utm_medium=ad&amp;utm_source=google)</p>\n<p>Amjad Masad</p>\n<p>CEO and Co-founder, Replit</p>\n<p>* [ Twitter ](https://x.com/amasad)</p>\n<p>* [ Linkedin ](https://www.linkedin.com/in/amjadmasad/)</p>\n<p>Amjad Masad is CEO and co-founder of Replit, a company that makes coding accessible to billions on the planet, from children creating school projects to enterprises running their business. Prior to Replit, Amjad worked at Meta, where he led the JavaScript infrastructure team and contributed to popular open source developer tools. In addition, he played a key role as a founding engineer at Codecademy, a prominent online coding school.</p>\n<p>#### Amjad&#039;s Sessions</p>\n<p>##### Everyone Can Build Software Now. What Happens Next?</p>\n<p>AI is making it possible for millions of people to create software for the first time. Replit sits at the center of that shift. Join founder and CEO Amjad Masad for a conversation about the future of programming, entrepreneurship, and what happens when anyone can turn an idea into a product.</p>\n<p>October 15  15:15PM – 15:45PM America/Los_Angeles  Disrupt Stage  Developer Tools</p>\n<p>[ View in Agenda ](https://techcrunch.com/events/techcrunch-disrupt/agenda/#session-everyone-can-build-software-now-what-happens-next?promo=Disrupt25&amp;utm_campaign=disrupt2026&amp;utm_content=addiscount&amp;utm_medium=ad&amp;utm_source=google)</p>\n<p>Toni Schneider</p>\n<p>CEO, Bluesky Social</p>\n<p>* [ Twitter ](https://bsky.app/profile/toni.bsky.team)</p>\n<p>* [ Linkedin ](https://www.linkedin.com/in/tonischneider/)</p>\n<p>Toni Schneider is Chief Executive Officer of Bluesky, the company building the future of the open social web. He was previously the founding CEO of Automattic, the company behind WordPress.com, and has been a partner at True Ventures since 2005.</p>\n<p>#### Toni&#039;s Sessions</p>\n<p>##### Can Social Start Over?</p>\n<p>Social media is entering a new era. With a new leadership team at the helm, Bluesky is betting that openness, user choice, and new technology can reshape how online communities are built. Join CEO Toni Schneider and COO Rose Wang for a conversation about the future of social media, competing with established platforms, and what it takes to build the next generation of consumer technology.</p>\n<p>October 15  10:30AM – 11:00AM America/Los_Angeles  Disrupt Stage  Consumer Technology</p>\n<p>[ View in Agenda ](https://techcrunch.com/events/techcrunch-disrupt/agenda/#session-can-social-start-over?promo=Disrupt25&amp;utm_campaign=disrupt2026&amp;utm_content=addiscount&amp;utm_medium=ad&amp;utm_source=google)</p>\n<p>Don’t miss this speaker – secure your spot today.</p>\n<p>Some areas of this page may shift around if you resize the browser window. Be sure to check heading and document order.</p>\n\n\n<hr class=\"news-scraper-divider\"><p class=\"text-muted news-scraper-source\"><small><em>Source: <a href=\"https://techcrunch.com/events/techcrunch-disrupt/?utm_source=google&amp;utm_medium=ad&amp;utm_campaign=disrupt2026&amp;utm_content=addiscount&amp;promo=Disrupt25&amp;display=true\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">https://techcrunch.com/events/techcrunch-disrupt/?utm_source=google&amp;utm_medium=ad&amp;utm_campaign=disrupt2026&amp;utm_content=addiscount&amp;promo=Disrupt25&amp;display=true</a></em></small></p>','Financial News Intelligence','','draft','open','open','','','','','2026-09-24 09:06:51','0000-00-00 00:00:00','',0,'http://localhost/financial/?p=11',0,'post','',0),(12,1,'2026-09-24 09:06:53','0000-00-00 00:00:00','<p>[Skip to content](https://techcrunch.com/brand-studio/#wp--skip-link--target)</p>\n<p>–:–:–:–</p>\n<p>Disrupt 2026: OpenAI, Anthropic, Replit, and more take over 6 industry stages. [25% off tickets now](https://techcrunch.com/events/techcrunch-disrupt/?utm_source=google&amp;utm_medium=ad&amp;utm_campaign=disrupt2026&amp;utm_content=addiscount&amp;promo=Disrupt25&amp;display=true)</p>\n<p>Disrupt ticket savings of up to $200 + 50% off a second ends Sept 25, 11:59 p.m. PT.</p>\n<p>Close</p>\n<h3>TechCrunch Brand Studio</h3>\n<p>**We create brand content and experiences that reach millions of entrepreneurs, c-suite executives, venture capitalists, and tech-obsessed readers.**</p>\n<p>**Leaning on TechCrunch’s proven record of profiling startups, reviewing new products, and breaking tech news, TC Brand Studio will help give your brand the platform to reach the right audience.**</p>\n<h3>What We Do</h3>\n<h3>Create</h3>\n<p>Custom and hosted content authentic to TechCrunch that delivers on brand objectives.</p>\n<h3>Connect</h3>\n<p>Reach millions of unique monthly readers made up of highly engaged and voracious tech consumers.</p>\n<h3>Amplify</h3>\n<p>Expand your reach through content distributed across TechCrunch, social media, podcasts, and newsletters.</p>\n<h3>Featured Works</h3>\n<h3>Contact Us</h3>\n<p>Some areas of this page may shift around if you resize the browser window. Be sure to check heading and document order.</p>\n<p>advertisement</p>\n<p>advertisement</p>\n<p>advertisement</p>\n\n\n<hr class=\"news-scraper-divider\"><p class=\"text-muted news-scraper-source\"><small><em>Source: <a href=\"https://techcrunch.com/brand-studio/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">https://techcrunch.com/brand-studio/</a></em></small></p>','TechCrunch Brand Studio','','draft','open','open','','','','','2026-09-24 09:06:53','0000-00-00 00:00:00','',0,'http://localhost/financial/?p=12',0,'post','',0),(13,1,'2026-09-24 09:06:55','0000-00-00 00:00:00','<p>[Skip to content](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/23/enveda-secures-311m-to-bring-more-nature-derived-ai-drugs-into-clinical-trials/#wp--skip-link--target)</p>\n<p>–:–:–:–</p>\n<p>Disrupt 2026: OpenAI, Anthropic, Replit, and more take over 6 industry stages. [25% off tickets now](https://techcrunch.com/events/techcrunch-disrupt/?utm_source=google&amp;utm_medium=ad&amp;utm_campaign=disrupt2026&amp;utm_content=addiscount&amp;promo=Disrupt25&amp;display=true)</p>\n<p>Disrupt ticket savings of up to $200 + 50% off a second ends Sept 25, 11:59 p.m. PT.</p>\n<p>Close</p>\n<p>In Brief</p>\n<p>Posted:</p>\n<p>12:31 PM PDT · September 23, 2026</p>\n<p>**Image Credits:** Viswa Colluru / Enveda</p>\n<h3>Enveda secures $311M to bring more nature-derived AI drugs into clinical trials</h3>\n<p>[Enveda](https://techcrunch.com/2024/06/14/enveda-raises-55m-to-combine-ancient-remedies-with-ai-for-drug-discovery/), a biotech startup that uses AI to discover new drugs in the natural world, has raised a $311 million Series E at a $2 billion valuation. The fresh funding, which was led by Catalio Capital Management, with participation from Iconiq and others, doubles the valuation Enveda achieved 12 months ago.</p>\n<p>The idea behind Enveda is that powerful medicines could be harnessed from plants and microbes, rather than synthesized from scratch in a lab. Viswa Colluru, an early employee of Recursion Pharmaceuticals, founded Enveda in 2019 to speed up the process of discovering these new drugs using AI and other techniques.</p>\n<p>While AI hasn’t yet produced any FDA-approved drugs, Enveda is among the wave of companies advancing AI-discovered candidates into human clinical trials. The startup is currently testing several drugs in patients, including one targeting severe skin conditions and another designed to help maintain weight loss after stopping GLP-1s.</p>\n<p>Topics</p>\n<p>[AI](https://techcrunch.com/category/artificial-intelligence/), [Biotech &amp; Health](https://techcrunch.com/category/biotech-health/), [clinical trials](https://techcrunch.com/tag/clinical-trials/), [Enveda](https://techcrunch.com/tag/enveda/), [In Brief](https://techcrunch.com/tag/in-brief/), [Startups](https://techcrunch.com/category/startups/)</p>\n<p>October 13 – 15</p>\n<p>San Francisco</p>\n<p>**Your next big connection is at Disrupt.** Connect with 10,000+ founders, VCs, operators, and tech leaders. Explore tomorrow’s breakthroughs, hear what’s shaping tech today, and **save up to $200 by Sept. 25 at 11:59 p.m. PT.**</p>\n<h3>Newsletters</h3>\n<p>Subscribe for the industry’s biggest tech news</p>\n<h3>Related</h3>\n<p>* [TechCrunch Disrupt 2026](https://techcrunch.com/events/techcrunch-disrupt/)</p>\n<h3>[StrictlyVC at TechCrunch Disrupt 2026: Inside the changing rules of venture capital](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/23/strictlyvc-at-techcrunch-disrupt-2026-inside-the-changing-rules-of-venture-capital/)</h3>\n<p>18 hours ago</p>\n<p>* ###  [Snorkel AI triples valuation to $3.5B as demand for AI training data booms](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/22/snorkel-ai-triples-valuation-to-3-5b-as-demand-for-ai-training-data-booms/)</p>\n<p>1 day ago</p>\n<p>* ###  [Nscale’s IPO will test Wall Street’s appetite for concentrated AI bets once again](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/22/nscales-ipo-will-test-wall-streets-appetite-for-concentrated-ai-bets-once-again/)</p>\n<p>2 days ago</p>\n<h3>Latest in Biotech &amp; Health</h3>\n<p>* ###  [Anthropic says its biology lab has already found something big](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/23/anthropic-says-its-biology-lab-has-already-found-something-big/)</p>\n<p>11 hours ago</p>\n<p>* In Brief</p>\n<h3>[Enveda secures $311M to bring more nature-derived AI drugs into clinical trials](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/23/enveda-secures-311m-to-bring-more-nature-derived-ai-drugs-into-clinical-trials/)</h3>\n<p>13 hours ago</p>\n<p>* [TechCrunch Disrupt 2026](https://techcrunch.com/events/techcrunch-disrupt/)</p>\n<h3>[3 days left to save up to $200 and make impactful connections at TechCrunch Disrupt 2026](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/23/3-days-left-to-save-up-to-200-and-make-impactful-connections-at-techcrunch-disrupt-2026/)</h3>\n<p>19 hours ago</p>\n<p>Some areas of this page may shift around if you resize the browser window. Be sure to check heading and document order.</p>\n\n\n<hr class=\"news-scraper-divider\"><p class=\"text-muted news-scraper-source\"><small><em>Source: <a href=\"https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/23/enveda-secures-311m-to-bring-more-nature-derived-ai-drugs-into-clinical-trials/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/23/enveda-secures-311m-to-bring-more-nature-derived-ai-drugs-into-clinical-trials/</a></em></small></p>','Enveda secures $311M to bring more nature-derived AI drugs into clinical trials','','draft','open','open','','','','','2026-09-24 09:06:55','0000-00-00 00:00:00','',0,'http://localhost/financial/?p=13',0,'post','',0),(14,1,'2026-09-24 09:06:57','0000-00-00 00:00:00','<p>[Skip to content](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/23/modal-motors-is-trying-to-cut-china-out-of-electric-motors-entirely/#wp--skip-link--target)</p>\n<p>–:–:–:–</p>\n<p>Disrupt 2026: OpenAI, Anthropic, Replit, and more take over 6 industry stages. [25% off tickets now](https://techcrunch.com/events/techcrunch-disrupt/?utm_source=google&amp;utm_medium=ad&amp;utm_campaign=disrupt2026&amp;utm_content=addiscount&amp;promo=Disrupt25&amp;display=true)</p>\n<p>Disrupt ticket savings of up to $200 + 50% off a second ends Sept 25, 11:59 p.m. PT.</p>\n<p>Close</p>\n<p>**Image Credits:** Modal Motors</p>\n<h3>Modal Motors is trying to cut China out of electric motors entirely</h3>\n<p>12:05 PM PDT · September 23, 2026</p>\n<p>Lately, it seems like everyone is talking about bringing manufacturing back to the United States, especially when it comes to hard tech like drones and robotics. Michael Van Steenburg thinks he’s ahead of the game.</p>\n<p>Van Steenburg spent the last decade building all kinds of electric motors in the U.S. and North America, and now he wants to do it again with his new startup, [Modal Motors](https://www.modalmotors.com/). This time, he wants to ditch rare-earth magnets entirely, in a bid to further separate the U.S. from China’s supply chain.</p>\n<p>As one of the founders in Startup Battlefield 200 who will compete at TechCrunch Disrupt next month, he thinks it’s an urgent pitch to make.</p>\n<p>“China’s already flexed its muscles a couple times,” Van Steenburg said, referring to the [country restricting access to rare-earth products](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-09-21/china-rare-earth-magnet-exports-to-us-drop-before-xi-trump-meet).</p>\n<p>There are other companies working on setting up electric motor manufacturing and supply chains in the U.S., but Van Steenburg argues they’re doing it the wrong way by continuing to rely on rare-earth magnets. Domestic supply of those materials is still years away, he said.</p>\n<p>“Other people are ‘reshoring,’ [but] they’re basically knocking off Chinese motors and just doing it in the U.S. But they’re hoping that the U.S. rare-earth magnet system is going to get up to speed fast enough,” he said. “It’s going to still be a while longer before they can actually replace China.”</p>\n<p>Modal Motors has two products that it’s developing ahead of Disrupt. One is a small electric motor meant for drones that can also work in fans, blowers, and even actuators. It’s designed to “minimize mass while delivering maximum torque density and efficiency,” according to the startup’s website.</p>\n<p>The more radical design is what Modal refers to as the “Hybrid Enigma Drive,” a planar — or flat — electric motor that can fit in lower-profile spaces.</p>\n<p>Van Steenburg is drawing on decades of experience in the automotive industry to develop these motors and get them customer-ready. But there’s also an irony in the work Van Steenburg is doing to make Modal Motors’ products a China-free option; in the late 1990s, he worked on a hybrid gas-electric drivetrain that China wanted to put in its [so-called “People’s Car.”](https://www.nytimes.com/1994/09/22/business/china-is-planning-a-people-s-car.html)</p>\n<p>“It’s kind of comical,” he said, thinking back on that experience and how much the world has changed. What hasn’t changed, he said, is his outsider streak and strong conviction about doing things that are hard.</p>\n<p>“I always tell people that the clock started ticking 25 years ago, and in that whole time, I’ve watched a slow-motion train wreck as China’s been going around the world, grabbing mines, grabbing mineral rights, doing all these things, and slowly but surely monopolizing the entire rare-earth supply chain,” he said.</p>\n<p>Topics</p>\n<p>[electric motors](https://techcrunch.com/tag/electric-motors/), [Startup Battlefield 200](https://techcrunch.com/tag/startup-battlefield-200/), [Startups](https://techcrunch.com/category/startups/), [TechCrunch Disrupt](https://techcrunch.com/tag/techcrunch-disrupt/)</p>\n<p>_When you purchase through links in our articles,[we may earn a small commission](https://techcrunch.com/techcrunch-affiliate-monetization-standards/). This doesn’t affect our editorial independence._</p>\n<p>Sean O&#039;Kane</p>\n<p>Sr. Reporter, Transportation</p>\n<p>Sean O’Kane is a reporter who has spent a decade covering the rapidly-evolving business and technology of the transportation industry, including Tesla and the many startups chasing Elon Musk. Most recently, he was a reporter at Bloomberg News where he helped break stories about some of the most notorious EV SPAC flops. He previously worked at The Verge, where he also covered consumer technology, hosted many short- and long-form videos, performed product and editorial photography, and once nearly passed out in a Red Bull Air Race plane.</p>\n<p>You can contact or verify outreach from Sean by emailing sean.okane@techcrunch.com or via encrypted message at okane.01 on Signal.</p>\n<p>October 13 – 15</p>\n<p>San Francisco</p>\n<p>**Your next big connection is at Disrupt.** Connect with 10,000+ founders, VCs, operators, and tech leaders. Explore tomorrow’s breakthroughs, hear what’s shaping tech today, and **save up to $200 by Sept. 25 at 11:59 p.m. PT.**</p>\n<h3>Most Popular</h3>\n<p>* [PitPro’s first tire-changing robot goes live in Canada](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/23/pitpros-first-tire-changing-robot-goes-live-in-canada/)</p>\n<p>* [Anthropic releases Opus 5.5 with lower prices and Fable-level performance](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/22/anthropic-releases-opus-5-5-with-lower-prices-and-fable-level-performance/)</p>\n<p>* [Meta’s Muse is outpacing ChatGPT’s early mobile launch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/21/metas-muse-is-outpacing-chatgpts-early-mobile-launch/)</p>\n<p>* [Tilly Norwood’s press tour is going about as well as you’d expect for an AI](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/18/tilly-norwoods-press-tour-is-going-about-as-well-as-youd-expect-for-an-ai/)</p>\n<p>* [A new kind of AI model from a ChatGPT inventor is thrilling developers](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/18/a-new-kind-of-ai-model-from-a-chatgpt-inventor-is-thrilling-developers/)</p>\n<p>* [OpenAI caught its models leaving notes to successors to hide bad behavior](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/17/openai-caught-its-models-leaving-notes-to-successors-to-hide-bad-behavior/)</p>\n<p>* [Microsoft exec called AI scraping ‘the largest theft of labor in human history,’ new unredacted filings reveal](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/17/microsoft-exec-called-ai-scraping-the-largest-theft-of-labor-in-human-history-new-unredacted-filings-reveal/)</p>\n<p>Loading the next article</p>\n<p>Error loading the next article</p>\n<p>Some areas of this page may shift around if you resize the browser window. Be sure to check heading and document order.</p>\n\n\n<hr class=\"news-scraper-divider\"><p class=\"text-muted news-scraper-source\"><small><em>Source: <a href=\"https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/23/modal-motors-is-trying-to-cut-china-out-of-electric-motors-entirely/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/23/modal-motors-is-trying-to-cut-china-out-of-electric-motors-entirely/</a></em></small></p>','Modal Motors is trying to cut China out of electric motors entirely','','draft','open','open','','','','','2026-09-24 09:06:57','0000-00-00 00:00:00','',0,'http://localhost/financial/?p=14',0,'post','',0),(15,1,'2026-09-24 09:06:59','0000-00-00 00:00:00','<p>[Skip to content](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/23/strictlyvc-at-techcrunch-disrupt-2026-inside-the-changing-rules-of-venture-capital/#wp--skip-link--target)</p>\n<p>–:–:–:–</p>\n<p>Disrupt 2026: OpenAI, Anthropic, Replit, and more take over 6 industry stages. [25% off tickets now](https://techcrunch.com/events/techcrunch-disrupt/?utm_source=google&amp;utm_medium=ad&amp;utm_campaign=disrupt2026&amp;utm_content=addiscount&amp;promo=Disrupt25&amp;display=true)</p>\n<p>Disrupt ticket savings of up to $200 + 50% off a second ends Sept 25, 11:59 p.m. PT.</p>\n<p>Close</p>\n<p>**Image Credits:** Aaron V Barrera Photography</p>\n<h3>StrictlyVC at TechCrunch Disrupt 2026: Inside the changing rules of venture capital</h3>\n<p>7:30 AM PDT · September 23, 2026</p>\n<p>Thanks largely to AI and the rate at which startups are scaling, venture capital is in a state of flux, from who provides the money to how it gets deployed and what happens when companies are finally ready to go public. at [**TechCrunch Disrupt 2026**](https://techcrunch.com/events/techcrunch-disrupt/?utm_source=tc&amp;utm_medium=post&amp;utm_campaign=disrupt2026&amp;utm_content=agenda&amp;promo=post_svcagenda_09232026&amp;display=), happening at San Francisco’s Moscone West on October 13-15, will bring together investors, institutional LPs, family office managers, and market experts for an afternoon of candid conversations about where capital is moving, how the money managers behind those venture dollars feel about the feverish dealmaking out there, and what founders and VCs need to understand about the new funding landscape.</p>\n<p>The only way to lean into these exclusive deep-dive sessions is with a Disrupt [Investor Pass](https://techcrunch.com/events/techcrunch-disrupt/tickets/?promo=post_svcagenda_09232026&amp;utm_campaign=disrupt2026&amp;utm_content=agenda&amp;utm_medium=post&amp;utm_source=tc). Built for investors, the StrictlyVC sessions offer a closer look at the fast-changing landscape and the forces shaping what’s next. [**Register by September 25 at 11:59 p.m. PT to save $200 on your Investor Pass.**](https://techcrunch.com/events/techcrunch-disrupt/tickets/?promo=post_svcagenda_09232026&amp;utm_campaign=disrupt2026&amp;utm_content=agenda&amp;utm_medium=post&amp;utm_source=tc)</p>\n<h3>Explore the StrictlyVC agenda</h3>\n<p>**October 14** 3:00–3:45 p.m. PT | Networking, Drinks &amp; Light Bites3:45–4:50 p.m.PT | StrictlyVC Conversations4:50–6:00 p.m. PT | Drinks &amp; Networking</p>\n<h3>Start with networking</h3>\n<p>Join us at 3:00 p.m. PT for drinks and light bites, with 45 minutes to connect with fellow investors, founders, and venture leaders before the conversations begin. After the program wraps, stick around for more drinks and more mingling.</p>\n<h3>Dive into the candid sessions</h3>\n<p>Visit the [**Disrupt agenda**](https://techcrunch.com/events/techcrunch-disrupt/agenda/?promo=post_svcagenda_09232026&amp;utm_campaign=disrupt2026&amp;utm_content=agenda&amp;utm_medium=post&amp;utm_source=tc) to learn about all the sessions and speakers for StrictlyVC, plus the 200+ sessions across all six industry stages, roundtables, and breakouts.</p>\n<p>**Image Credits:** Slava Blazer Photography / TechCrunch</p>\n<p>#### The New Rules of Going Public</p>\n<p>**[Ryan Flanagan](https://www.linkedin.com/in/ryan-flanagan-1490704/), ICR**</p>\n<p>The IPO window is reopening, but the playbook has changed. Founders and investors face a tougher path to the public markets, with higher expectations around growth, governance, and credibility. Join [ICR](http://icrinc.com/)‘s Ryan Flanagan for a candid conversation on what makes a company IPO-ready today, the decisions that matter years before a listing, and how to position a business for a successful exit in a more disciplined market.</p>\n<p>#### The New Power Players: How Family Offices Are Reshaping Venture Investing</p>\n<p>**[Bruce K Lee](https://www.linkedin.com/in/bruce-k-lee-abb169168/), Keebeck Capital Management; and [Dave Sachse](https://www.linkedin.com/in/dave-sachse-1244989/), Sachse Family Fund**</p>\n<p>Family offices have become one of the fastest-growing sources of startup capital, often moving faster and investing with greater flexibility than traditional institutions, but also, at times, piling in at the wrong times. Join prominent family office investors as they discuss how they’re approaching venture today, where they’re placing long-term bets, how they work alongside VCs, and why founders increasingly see them as strategic partners.</p>\n<p>#### What Limited Partners Want Now</p>\n<p>**[Amit Bhatti](https://www.linkedin.com/in/amit-s-bhatti-1o1/), TrueBridge Capital Partners; and [Beezer Clarkson](https://www.linkedin.com/in/elizabethclarkson/), LGT Capital Partners**</p>\n<p>Venture firms are competing harder than ever for institutional capital, while limited partners are rethinking everything from manager selection to concentrated AI exposure and liquidity expectations. Hear from leading LPs as they discuss what they’re looking for in today’s market, how they’re evaluating emerging managers versus established firms, and where they believe the next generation of venture returns will come from.</p>\n<h3>Secure your Investor Pass and $200 savings</h3>\n<p>[**TechCrunch Disrupt 2026**](https://techcrunch.com/events/techcrunch-disrupt/?utm_source=tc&amp;utm_medium=post&amp;utm_campaign=disrupt2026&amp;utm_content=agenda&amp;promo=post_svcagenda_09232026&amp;display=) brings together 10,000+ founders, VCs, and operators from across the global tech ecosystem to make connections that can shape what’s next, discover emerging breakthroughs, and hear from leaders driving the industry forward. This is where big ideas converge and new opportunities take shape. [**Register for your Investor Pass now to save up to $200**](https://techcrunch.com/events/techcrunch-disrupt/tickets/?promo=post_svcagenda_09232026&amp;utm_campaign=disrupt2026&amp;utm_content=agenda&amp;utm_medium=post&amp;utm_source=tc) before September 25 at 11:59 p.m. PT. Join Disrupt in San Francisco on October 13-15.</p>\n<p>Topics</p>\n<p>[AI](https://techcrunch.com/category/artificial-intelligence/), [Fundraising](https://techcrunch.com/category/fundraising/), [Startups](https://techcrunch.com/category/startups/), [StrictlyVC](https://techcrunch.com/tag/strictlyvc/), [TechCrunch Disrupt](https://techcrunch.com/tag/techcrunch-disrupt/), [Venture](https://techcrunch.com/category/venture/)</p>\n<p>_When you purchase through links in our articles,[we may earn a small commission](https://techcrunch.com/techcrunch-affiliate-monetization-standards/). This doesn’t affect our editorial independence._</p>\n<p>TechCrunch Events</p>\n<p>October 13 – 15</p>\n<p>San Francisco</p>\n<p>**Your next big connection is at Disrupt.** Connect with 10,000+ founders, VCs, operators, and tech leaders. Explore tomorrow’s breakthroughs, hear what’s shaping tech today, and **save up to $200 by Sept. 25 at 11:59 p.m. PT.**</p>\n<h3>Most Popular</h3>\n<p>* [PitPro’s first tire-changing robot goes live in Canada](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/23/pitpros-first-tire-changing-robot-goes-live-in-canada/)</p>\n<p>* [Anthropic releases Opus 5.5 with lower prices and Fable-level performance](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/22/anthropic-releases-opus-5-5-with-lower-prices-and-fable-level-performance/)</p>\n<p>* [Meta’s Muse is outpacing ChatGPT’s early mobile launch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/21/metas-muse-is-outpacing-chatgpts-early-mobile-launch/)</p>\n<p>* [Tilly Norwood’s press tour is going about as well as you’d expect for an AI](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/18/tilly-norwoods-press-tour-is-going-about-as-well-as-youd-expect-for-an-ai/)</p>\n<p>* [A new kind of AI model from a ChatGPT inventor is thrilling developers](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/18/a-new-kind-of-ai-model-from-a-chatgpt-inventor-is-thrilling-developers/)</p>\n<p>* [OpenAI caught its models leaving notes to successors to hide bad behavior](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/17/openai-caught-its-models-leaving-notes-to-successors-to-hide-bad-behavior/)</p>\n<p>* [Microsoft exec called AI scraping ‘the largest theft of labor in human history,’ new unredacted filings reveal](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/17/microsoft-exec-called-ai-scraping-the-largest-theft-of-labor-in-human-history-new-unredacted-filings-reveal/)</p>\n<p>Loading the next article</p>\n<p>Error loading the next article</p>\n<p>Some areas of this page may shift around if you resize the browser window. Be sure to check heading and document order.</p>\n\n\n<hr class=\"news-scraper-divider\"><p class=\"text-muted news-scraper-source\"><small><em>Source: <a href=\"https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/23/strictlyvc-at-techcrunch-disrupt-2026-inside-the-changing-rules-of-venture-capital/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/23/strictlyvc-at-techcrunch-disrupt-2026-inside-the-changing-rules-of-venture-capital/</a></em></small></p>','StrictlyVC at TechCrunch Disrupt 2026: Inside the changing rules of venture capital','','draft','open','open','','','','','2026-09-24 09:06:59','0000-00-00 00:00:00','',0,'http://localhost/financial/?p=15',0,'post','',0),(16,1,'2026-09-24 09:07:02','0000-00-00 00:00:00','<p>[Skip to content](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/23/3-days-left-to-save-up-to-200-and-make-impactful-connections-at-techcrunch-disrupt-2026/#wp--skip-link--target)</p>\n<p>–:–:–:–</p>\n<p>Disrupt 2026: OpenAI, Anthropic, Replit, and more take over 6 industry stages. [25% off tickets now](https://techcrunch.com/events/techcrunch-disrupt/?utm_source=google&amp;utm_medium=ad&amp;utm_campaign=disrupt2026&amp;utm_content=addiscount&amp;promo=Disrupt25&amp;display=true)</p>\n<p>Disrupt ticket savings of up to $200 + 50% off a second ends Sept 25, 11:59 p.m. PT.</p>\n<p>Close</p>\n<p>**Image Credits:** TechCrunch</p>\n<h3>3 days left to save up to $200 and make impactful connections at TechCrunch Disrupt 2026</h3>\n<p>7:00 AM PDT · September 23, 2026</p>\n<p>There are now three days left to save up to $200 on your [**TechCrunch Disrupt 2026**](https://techcrunch.com/events/techcrunch-disrupt/?utm_source=tc&amp;utm_medium=post&amp;utm_campaign=disrupt2026&amp;utm_content=ticketsales&amp;promo=post_rbplus3days_09232026&amp;display=) ticket before prices increase. As we continue our countdown of the five reasons why you should attend, we arrive at a key reason that brings 10,000+ founders, investors, and operators together at San Francisco’s Moscone West on October 13-15.</p>\n<p>Don’t miss out on securing your ticket savings before they vanish on September 25 at 11:59 p.m. PT. [**Register to grab yours and get a second pass at 50% off.**](https://techcrunch.com/events/techcrunch-disrupt/tickets/?promo=post_rbplus3days_09232026&amp;utm_campaign=disrupt2026&amp;utm_content=ticketsales&amp;utm_medium=post&amp;utm_source=tc)</p>\n<h3>Reason No. 3: Connections</h3>\n<p>The right introduction can change what happens next for a company. It might lead to your next investor. A customer you’ve been trying to reach. A future hire. A technology partner. Or simply someone solving a problem close enough to yours that the conversation changes how you think about it. At TechCrunch Disrupt 2026, those people are concentrated in one place.</p>\n<h3>Turn 10,000+ attendees into the people who’ll actually push the needle</h3>\n<p>Big events create possibilities. The trick is making them useful. At Disrupt, you can schedule 1:1 meetings through the TechCrunch Events app, which provides AI-powered matchmaking, and you can use the dedicated Networking Lounge across all three days. Alongside that are industry meetups, [**roundtable sessions**](https://techcrunch.com/events/techcrunch-disrupt/agenda/?promo=post_rbplus3days_09232026&amp;utm_campaign=disrupt2026&amp;utm_content=ticketsales&amp;utm_medium=post&amp;utm_source=tc), and the conversations that happen when thousands of people building, funding, and scaling technology companies move through the same space. Plus, countless impromptu meetings can happen from running into the right person, anywhere in the venue.</p>\n<p>[**Register here to start making intentional connections**](https://techcrunch.com/events/techcrunch-disrupt/tickets/?promo=post_rbplus3days_09232026&amp;utm_campaign=disrupt2026&amp;utm_content=ticketsales&amp;utm_medium=post&amp;utm_source=tc) and save up to $200 before your savings disappear on September 25 at 11:59 p.m. PT.</p>\n<p>**Image Credits:** Halo Creative</p>\n<h3>Go beyond the LinkedIn profile</h3>\n<p>There’s a difference between knowing who someone is and actually having a conversation with them. A 15-minute meeting can tell you more about a founder than weeks of following their company online. A conversation with an investor can help you understand what they are really looking for. Meeting another operator can surface an answer to a challenge you assumed was unique to your team.</p>\n<p>And sometimes the useful conversation is the one you didn’t schedule. The founder sitting next to you before a session. The investor asking the question you were thinking about. The person you meet after a roundtable because you’re both trying to solve the same problem. That proximity matters.</p>\n<h3>Make three days work for you</h3>\n<p>You could spend months trying to arrange the individual conversations that become possible over three days at Disrupt. So don’t just build a session schedule. Build a networking schedule.</p>\n<p>Identify who you want to meet using the event app. Book your one-on-ones. Leave space for the unexpected. Walk the [**Expo Hall**](https://techcrunch.com/events/techcrunch-disrupt/exhibit/?promo=post_rbplus3days_09232026&amp;utm_campaign=disrupt2026&amp;utm_content=ticketsales&amp;utm_medium=post&amp;utm_source=tc). Stay for the meetups. Talk to the person standing next to you, because one useful conversation can outlast the entire event.</p>\n<p>**Image Credits:** Eric Slomonson, The Photo Group</p>\n<h3>3 days left to save up to $200</h3>\n<p>Current [**TechCrunch Disrupt 2026**](https://techcrunch.com/events/techcrunch-disrupt/?utm_source=tc&amp;utm_medium=post&amp;utm_campaign=disrupt2026&amp;utm_content=ticketsales&amp;promo=post_rbplus3days_09232026&amp;display=) ticket savings end September 25 at 11:59 p.m. PT.</p>\n<p>[**Register now to save up to $200**](https://techcrunch.com/events/techcrunch-disrupt/tickets/?promo=post_rbplus3days_09232026&amp;utm_campaign=disrupt2026&amp;utm_content=ticketsales&amp;utm_medium=post&amp;utm_source=tc) on your ticket and [**get a second ticket of the same type at 50% off**](https://techcrunch.com/events/techcrunch-disrupt/tickets/?promo=post_rbplus3days_09232026&amp;utm_campaign=disrupt2026&amp;utm_content=ticketsales&amp;utm_medium=post&amp;utm_source=tc). Or bring a group of four or more to save up to 30%.</p>\n<p>Topics</p>\n<p>[AI](https://techcrunch.com/category/artificial-intelligence/), [Biotech &amp; Health](https://techcrunch.com/category/biotech-health/), [Climate](https://techcrunch.com/category/climate/), [Fintech](https://techcrunch.com/category/fintech/), [Fundraising](https://techcrunch.com/category/fundraising/), [Robotics](https://techcrunch.com/category/robotics/), [Startups](https://techcrunch.com/category/startups/), [TC](https://techcrunch.com/category/tc/), [TechCrunch Disrupt](https://techcrunch.com/tag/techcrunch-disrupt/), [Venture](https://techcrunch.com/category/venture/)</p>\n<p>_When you purchase through links in our articles,[we may earn a small commission](https://techcrunch.com/techcrunch-affiliate-monetization-standards/). This doesn’t affect our editorial independence._</p>\n<p>TechCrunch Events</p>\n<p>October 13 – 15</p>\n<p>San Francisco</p>\n<p>**Your next big connection is at Disrupt.** Connect with 10,000+ founders, VCs, operators, and tech leaders. Explore tomorrow’s breakthroughs, hear what’s shaping tech today, and **save up to $200 by Sept. 25 at 11:59 p.m. PT.**</p>\n<h3>Most Popular</h3>\n<p>* [PitPro’s first tire-changing robot goes live in Canada](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/23/pitpros-first-tire-changing-robot-goes-live-in-canada/)</p>\n<p>* [Anthropic releases Opus 5.5 with lower prices and Fable-level performance](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/22/anthropic-releases-opus-5-5-with-lower-prices-and-fable-level-performance/)</p>\n<p>* [Meta’s Muse is outpacing ChatGPT’s early mobile launch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/21/metas-muse-is-outpacing-chatgpts-early-mobile-launch/)</p>\n<p>* [Tilly Norwood’s press tour is going about as well as you’d expect for an AI](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/18/tilly-norwoods-press-tour-is-going-about-as-well-as-youd-expect-for-an-ai/)</p>\n<p>* [A new kind of AI model from a ChatGPT inventor is thrilling developers](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/18/a-new-kind-of-ai-model-from-a-chatgpt-inventor-is-thrilling-developers/)</p>\n<p>* [OpenAI caught its models leaving notes to successors to hide bad behavior](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/17/openai-caught-its-models-leaving-notes-to-successors-to-hide-bad-behavior/)</p>\n<p>* [Microsoft exec called AI scraping ‘the largest theft of labor in human history,’ new unredacted filings reveal](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/17/microsoft-exec-called-ai-scraping-the-largest-theft-of-labor-in-human-history-new-unredacted-filings-reveal/)</p>\n<p>Loading the next article</p>\n<p>Error loading the next article</p>\n<p>Some areas of this page may shift around if you resize the browser window. Be sure to check heading and document order.</p>\n<p>advertisement</p>\n<p>advertisement</p>\n<p>advertisement</p>\n\n\n<hr class=\"news-scraper-divider\"><p class=\"text-muted news-scraper-source\"><small><em>Source: <a href=\"https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/23/3-days-left-to-save-up-to-200-and-make-impactful-connections-at-techcrunch-disrupt-2026/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/23/3-days-left-to-save-up-to-200-and-make-impactful-connections-at-techcrunch-disrupt-2026/</a></em></small></p>','3 days left to save up to $200 and make impactful connections at TechCrunch Disrupt 2026','','draft','open','open','','','','','2026-09-24 09:07:02','0000-00-00 00:00:00','',0,'http://localhost/financial/?p=16',0,'post','',0),(17,1,'2026-09-24 09:07:04','0000-00-00 00:00:00','<p>[Skip to content](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/23/pitpros-first-tire-changing-robot-goes-live-in-canada/#wp--skip-link--target)</p>\n<p>–:–:–:–</p>\n<p>Disrupt 2026: OpenAI, Anthropic, Replit, and more take over 6 industry stages. [25% off tickets now](https://techcrunch.com/events/techcrunch-disrupt/?utm_source=google&amp;utm_medium=ad&amp;utm_campaign=disrupt2026&amp;utm_content=addiscount&amp;promo=Disrupt25&amp;display=true)</p>\n<p>Disrupt ticket savings of up to $200 + 50% off a second ends Sept 25, 11:59 p.m. PT.</p>\n<p>Close</p>\n<p>**Image Credits:** PitPro Automation</p>\n<h3>PitPro’s first tire-changing robot goes live in Canada</h3>\n<p>6:00 AM PDT · September 23, 2026</p>\n<p>If you live in Canada, this year’s set of snow tires might be installed by a robot.</p>\n<p>San Francisco startup [PitPro Automation](https://www.pitproauto.com/) said Wednesday that it has installed its first automatic tire-changing system at a tire shop in Calgary, Alberta. The startup says its robotic system, first revealed last year, can swap a full set of four tires in under 15 minutes. The first deployment is at a Kal Tire location — one of Canada’s largest independent tire service chains.</p>\n<p>PitPro is one of myriad startups trying to disrupt different parts of the car ownership experience. While founder and CEO Jeremy Conrad told TechCrunch he ultimately wants to move into other kinds of automated maintenance, he thinks there’s big opportunity to build a business around tires in the near term.</p>\n<p>Changing tires is “backbreaking” work, and tire shops are facing a major labor shortage, he said in an interview. Even when shops can find people for the job, he said, those workers often leave within a few months to a year.</p>\n<p>“One shop manager was telling me they had to get special permission to pay more because a Panda Express opened across the street and it took about half their employees,” he said.</p>\n<p>PitPro’s system is designed to slide right into existing maintenance bays. It features two boxy robots, each roughly the size of an oven, that use a mix of sensors and a robotic arm to locate and loosen the lug nuts. The robots store the lug nuts, pull off each wheel, and replace it with a new or rotated tire.</p>\n<p>PitPro has, to date, mostly talked up plans to deploy its tire-changing robots with commercial fleets. It’s still focused on that line of business, Conrad said, and the startup has former Ford Pro executive Ted Cannis as an adviser. (“He’s got relationships with basically kind of every major fleet in the United States, and he’s you know been very helpful in making introductions for us on that,” Conrad said.)</p>\n<p>But Conrad believes tire chains are a major opportunity as well, not just because of the labor shortage, but because of the constant demand for tire changes — especially in Canada, where switching to and from winter tires is a yearly occurrence for most car owners.</p>\n<p>Conrad said PitPro is focused on chains, rather than smaller shops, since they tend to have standardized layouts and offer greater potential for wider deployment down the road. On top of that, mechanics can still use the bay that PitPro’s tech occupies for other maintenance, increasing the chance that shops will be willing to try it out, Conrad said.</p>\n<p>The shift to real-world deployment has had its challenges, according to Conrad. Tire shops tend to operate with the bay doors open, which exposes PitPro’s robots to all kinds of dust, debris, and weather. The startup had to account for all that, not only to keep the system robust, but also to make sure the sensors maintain an accurate view of the tires.</p>\n<p>Not everything is a challenge, though. During the interview, Conrad shared a photo from the Calgary installation showing where the robots had been almost completely covered in an unexpected substance: dandelion seeds.</p>\n<p>“We looked into it, and it’s not going to be a problem for us,” Conrad laughed. “But it’s just like… I had never thought about that we might need to deal with a dust cloud of dandelions.”</p>\n<p>Topics</p>\n<p>[Robotics](https://techcrunch.com/category/robotics/), [Startups](https://techcrunch.com/category/startups/), [tires](https://techcrunch.com/tag/tires/), [Transportation](https://techcrunch.com/category/transportation/)</p>\n<p>_When you purchase through links in our articles,[we may earn a small commission](https://techcrunch.com/techcrunch-affiliate-monetization-standards/). This doesn’t affect our editorial independence._</p>\n<p>Sean O&#039;Kane</p>\n<p>Sr. Reporter, Transportation</p>\n<p>Sean O’Kane is a reporter who has spent a decade covering the rapidly-evolving business and technology of the transportation industry, including Tesla and the many startups chasing Elon Musk. Most recently, he was a reporter at Bloomberg News where he helped break stories about some of the most notorious EV SPAC flops. He previously worked at The Verge, where he also covered consumer technology, hosted many short- and long-form videos, performed product and editorial photography, and once nearly passed out in a Red Bull Air Race plane.</p>\n<p>You can contact or verify outreach from Sean by emailing sean.okane@techcrunch.com or via encrypted message at okane.01 on Signal.</p>\n<p>October 13 – 15</p>\n<p>San Francisco</p>\n<p>**Your next big connection is at Disrupt.** Connect with 10,000+ founders, VCs, operators, and tech leaders. Explore tomorrow’s breakthroughs, hear what’s shaping tech today, and **save up to $200 by Sept. 25 at 11:59 p.m. PT.**</p>\n<h3>Most Popular</h3>\n<p>* [PitPro’s first tire-changing robot goes live in Canada](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/23/pitpros-first-tire-changing-robot-goes-live-in-canada/)</p>\n<p>* [Anthropic releases Opus 5.5 with lower prices and Fable-level performance](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/22/anthropic-releases-opus-5-5-with-lower-prices-and-fable-level-performance/)</p>\n<p>* [Meta’s Muse is outpacing ChatGPT’s early mobile launch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/21/metas-muse-is-outpacing-chatgpts-early-mobile-launch/)</p>\n<p>* [Tilly Norwood’s press tour is going about as well as you’d expect for an AI](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/18/tilly-norwoods-press-tour-is-going-about-as-well-as-youd-expect-for-an-ai/)</p>\n<p>* [A new kind of AI model from a ChatGPT inventor is thrilling developers](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/18/a-new-kind-of-ai-model-from-a-chatgpt-inventor-is-thrilling-developers/)</p>\n<p>* [OpenAI caught its models leaving notes to successors to hide bad behavior](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/17/openai-caught-its-models-leaving-notes-to-successors-to-hide-bad-behavior/)</p>\n<p>* [Microsoft exec called AI scraping ‘the largest theft of labor in human history,’ new unredacted filings reveal](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/17/microsoft-exec-called-ai-scraping-the-largest-theft-of-labor-in-human-history-new-unredacted-filings-reveal/)</p>\n<p>Loading the next article</p>\n<p>Error loading the next article</p>\n<p>Some areas of this page may shift around if you resize the browser window. Be sure to check heading and document order.</p>\n\n\n<hr class=\"news-scraper-divider\"><p class=\"text-muted news-scraper-source\"><small><em>Source: <a href=\"https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/23/pitpros-first-tire-changing-robot-goes-live-in-canada/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/23/pitpros-first-tire-changing-robot-goes-live-in-canada/</a></em></small></p>','PitPro’s first tire-changing robot goes live in Canada','','draft','open','open','','','','','2026-09-24 09:07:04','0000-00-00 00:00:00','',0,'http://localhost/financial/?p=17',0,'post','',0),(18,1,'2026-09-24 09:07:06','0000-00-00 00:00:00','<p>[Skip to content](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/23/ema-raises-77m-as-ai-starts-eating-into-enterprise-software-and-services/#wp--skip-link--target)</p>\n<p>–:–:–:–</p>\n<p>Disrupt 2026: OpenAI, Anthropic, Replit, and more take over 6 industry stages. [25% off tickets now](https://techcrunch.com/events/techcrunch-disrupt/?utm_source=google&amp;utm_medium=ad&amp;utm_campaign=disrupt2026&amp;utm_content=addiscount&amp;promo=Disrupt25&amp;display=true)</p>\n<p>Disrupt ticket savings of up to $200 + 50% off a second ends Sept 25, 11:59 p.m. PT.</p>\n<p>Close</p>\n<p>**Image Credits:** Andriy Onufriyenko / Getty Images</p>\n<h3>Ema raises $77M as AI starts eating into enterprise software and services</h3>\n<p>5:00 AM PDT · September 23, 2026</p>\n<p>[Ema](https://www.ema.ai/), a startup that uses teams of AI agents to automate corporate processes across HR, IT, and finance, has raised $77 million in a new funding round as it aims to take on more of the work traditionally handled by enterprise software and IT services.</p>\n<p>The Series B round was led by Bengaluru-based venture firm Creaegis, with existing investors Accel, Section 32, and Prosus increasing their stakes. The financing brings the startup’s total funding to $140 million and more than quadruples its valuation from its last funding round in 2024. (Ema declined to disclose its latest valuation.) The round consisted entirely of primary equity, with no debt or secondary transactions, the startup confirmed to TechCrunch.</p>\n<p>The funding comes as AI is beginning to compete for dollars that businesses have traditionally spent on enterprise software and IT services. Startups, major AI labs, and established software companies are now fighting to capture that spending.</p>\n<p>[Founded in 2023](https://techcrunch.com/2024/03/05/ema-a-universal-ai-employee-emerges-from-stealth-with-25m/) by former Google and Coinbase executive Surojit Chatterjee and ex-Okta executive Souvik Sen, both based in the Bay Area, Ema is looking to expand its position in that market. The startup deploys its technology, which it calls “AI employees” — systems that coordinate multiple AI agents. These help carry out multi-step business processes across a company’s existing applications, rather than handling a single task at a time.</p>\n<p>Chatterjee sees that model eventually reducing companies’ reliance on traditional software products, including those sold as software-as-a-service (SaaS). Ema first “wraps” around an enterprise’s existing applications, he said, before customers can reduce their dependence on some of those products — and, in some cases, replace them altogether.</p>\n<p>“Many of our customers are already on the way to replace [large SaaS applications] completely, removing dependency on them, because they are mostly becoming like a database,” Chatterjee said.</p>\n<h3>Benefiting from AI labs’ push</h3>\n<p>In recent months, major AI companies have also pushed deeper into the enterprise market where Ema operates. [Anthropic](https://techcrunch.com/tag/anthropic/) has expanded efforts to bring Claude into companies’ core operations, including [financial](https://techcrunch.com/2026/02/24/anthropic-launches-new-push-for-enterprise-agents-with-plugins-for-finance-engineering-and-design/) and [legal](https://techcrunch.com/2026/05/12/the-ai-legal-services-industry-is-heating-up-anthropic-is-getting-in-on-the-action/) work. Similarly, [OpenAI](https://techcrunch.com/tag/openai/) has [established teams of forward-deployed engineers](https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/30/forward-deployed-engineers-are-the-ai-industrys-latest-talent-obsession/) who work alongside customers to put AI into production.</p>\n<p>Chatterjee, however, does not see the frontier AI labs as direct competitors. He told TechCrunch that Ema’s software can draw on more than 150 models, including frontier and open source models, while the startup focuses on the domain knowledge, integrations, and orchestration needed to automate business processes end-to-end.</p>\n<p>“Progress in frontier models is actually very beneficial to us,” Chatterjee stated.</p>\n<p>Ema’s approach is already gaining traction. The startup has more than 50 active enterprise deals, as well as over 1 million active enterprise users, and has handled more than 5 million actions and queries. Its customers include NTT DATA, Hitachi, ADP, PwC, Google, KPMG, Wipro, and Microsoft.</p>\n<p>Over the past two years, Ema said its revenue has grown 50-fold, while revenue bookings have surpassed $150 million. Chatterjee said the bookings figure includes the total value of multiyear contracts, including two- and three-year deals, rather than representing annual recurring revenue. He, however, declined to disclose the startup’s current annualized revenue run rate.</p>\n<p>Chatterjee told TechCrunch that more than 90% of Ema’s customers have expanded beyond their initial use case, with some deploying the technology across dozens of workflows. The startup’s net dollar retention rate is around 180%, he said, meaning existing customers are spending substantially more with Ema over time.</p>\n<p>Ema is also looking beyond the software itself. AI, Chatterjee said, can take over some of the implementation, integration, and consulting work that companies have traditionally paid IT services firms to perform around enterprise software.</p>\n<p>“A lot of the services companies are working with us,” Chatterjee said. “They are also dramatically changing or disrupting their own business models because they understand the human-forward model may not be the best model going forward.”</p>\n<p>Despite taking on work traditionally handled by software and services providers, Chatterjee said Ema has maintained gross margins of close to 80%. The startup, he noted, requires less human support as its AI systems learn from deployments, helping improve margins over time.</p>\n<p>Ema also does not charge customers based on software seats or the number of AI tokens they consume. Instead, Chatterjee said, its pricing is tied to the completion of tasks and business outcomes.</p>\n<p>Much of Ema’s new capital will go toward expanding its go-to-market operations, particularly sales and marketing, after spending its first years largely building the product, Chatterjee said. The Mountain View-headquartered startup has grown to nearly 200 employees and has its offices in Bengaluru, London, and Vancouver.</p>\n<p>Ema has so far focused primarily on customers in the U.S. and Europe. However, it now plans to expand into new markets over the next year, particularly across Asia-Pacific, South America, and parts of the Middle East.</p>\n<p>Topics</p>\n<p>[Accel](https://techcrunch.com/tag/accel/), [AI](https://techcrunch.com/category/artificial-intelligence/), [AI agents](https://techcrunch.com/tag/ai-agents/), [Creaegis](https://techcrunch.com/tag/creaegis/), [Ema](https://techcrunch.com/tag/ema/), [Enterprise](https://techcrunch.com/category/enterprise/), [India](https://techcrunch.com/region/asia/india/), [Prosus](https://techcrunch.com/tag/prosus/), [section 32](https://techcrunch.com/tag/section-32/), [Startups](https://techcrunch.com/category/startups/), [United States](https://techcrunch.com/region/north-america/united-states/)</p>\n<p>_When you purchase through links in our articles,[we may earn a small commission](https://techcrunch.com/techcrunch-affiliate-monetization-standards/). This doesn’t affect our editorial independence._</p>\n<p>Jagmeet Singh</p>\n<p>Reporter</p>\n<p>Jagmeet covers startups, tech policy-related updates, and all other major tech-centric developments from India for TechCrunch. He previously worked as a principal correspondent at NDTV.</p>\n<p>You can contact or verify outreach from Jagmeet by emailing mail@journalistjagmeet.com.</p>\n<p>October 13 – 15</p>\n<p>San Francisco</p>\n<p>**Your next big connection is at Disrupt.** Connect with 10,000+ founders, VCs, operators, and tech leaders. Explore tomorrow’s breakthroughs, hear what’s shaping tech today, and **save up to $200 by Sept. 25 at 11:59 p.m. PT.**</p>\n<h3>Most Popular</h3>\n<p>* [PitPro’s first tire-changing robot goes live in Canada](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/23/pitpros-first-tire-changing-robot-goes-live-in-canada/)</p>\n<p>* [Anthropic releases Opus 5.5 with lower prices and Fable-level performance](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/22/anthropic-releases-opus-5-5-with-lower-prices-and-fable-level-performance/)</p>\n<p>* [Meta’s Muse is outpacing ChatGPT’s early mobile launch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/21/metas-muse-is-outpacing-chatgpts-early-mobile-launch/)</p>\n<p>* [Tilly Norwood’s press tour is going about as well as you’d expect for an AI](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/18/tilly-norwoods-press-tour-is-going-about-as-well-as-youd-expect-for-an-ai/)</p>\n<p>* [A new kind of AI model from a ChatGPT inventor is thrilling developers](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/18/a-new-kind-of-ai-model-from-a-chatgpt-inventor-is-thrilling-developers/)</p>\n<p>* [OpenAI caught its models leaving notes to successors to hide bad behavior](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/17/openai-caught-its-models-leaving-notes-to-successors-to-hide-bad-behavior/)</p>\n<p>* [Microsoft exec called AI scraping ‘the largest theft of labor in human history,’ new unredacted filings reveal](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/17/microsoft-exec-called-ai-scraping-the-largest-theft-of-labor-in-human-history-new-unredacted-filings-reveal/)</p>\n<p>Loading the next article</p>\n<p>Error loading the next article</p>\n<p>Some areas of this page may shift around if you resize the browser window. Be sure to check heading and document order.</p>\n<p>sponsored</p>\n<p>sponsored</p>\n<p>sponsored</p>\n\n\n<hr class=\"news-scraper-divider\"><p class=\"text-muted news-scraper-source\"><small><em>Source: <a href=\"https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/23/ema-raises-77m-as-ai-starts-eating-into-enterprise-software-and-services/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/23/ema-raises-77m-as-ai-starts-eating-into-enterprise-software-and-services/</a></em></small></p>','Ema raises $77M as AI starts eating into enterprise software and services','','draft','open','open','','','','','2026-09-24 09:07:06','0000-00-00 00:00:00','',0,'http://localhost/financial/?p=18',0,'post','',0);
